    "innovation": "This paper operationalizes the novel Cost-Benefit-Risk Analysis (CBRA) framework, a hybrid approach that bridges target-based and trade-off-based decision-making by using a partial damage function to quantify how much precautionary risk in climate targets is captured by explicit economic losses.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MIND",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "modified FaIRv2.0",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "In this paper, we provide an improvement to the MIND model by updating the climate module, which provides a feedback mechanism between energy production choices and global mean temperature change. We replace the one-box climate model, originally introduced by Petschel-Held et al. (1999), with a modified version of FaIRv2.02 (Leach et al. 2021). As part of this update, we modified FaIR to include a tailored uncertainty scheme for climate sensitivity that allows the user to do probabilistic analysis and to assess the effects of learning about uncertain climate sensitivity, as detailed later in this section.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "FaIR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SUBSEQUENT_VERSION",
            "target_entity_instance": "FaIRv2.0",
            "target_entity_type": "Source",
            "paragraph": "The first version of FaIR was introduced by Millar et al. (2017). It builds on the four carbon compartments proposed by Joos et al. (2013), which were shown to effectively replicate the carbon dynamics of fully resolved climate systems. FaIR extends this framework by incorporating a feedback parameter that accounts for the saturation of carbon sinks, modifying the carbon retention time in each compartment. The total content of these compartments defines the atmospheric carbon concentration\u00b3, which is then translated into radiative forcing, driving temperature increases.\nTemperature dynamics in FaIR were initially modeled using two temperature compartments, following the framework proposed by Geoffroy et al. (2013). FaIRv2 (Leach et al. 2021) introduced a third temperature compartment and adjusted the feedback mechanism.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MIND",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "constant-elasticity-of-substitution (CES) function",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "The production function, representing the global economic output in terms of GDP, is modeled as a constant-elasticity-of-substitution (CES) function that depends on labor, capital, and energy as input factors.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "FaIR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Equilibrium Climate Sensitivity (ECS)",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "In our setup, only the equilibrium climate sensitivity (ECS) is varied directly, allowing its value to be adjusted freely rather than re-calibrating the entire parameter set required in full-ensemble approaches.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "FaIR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Transient Climate Response (TCR)",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "To ensure the model depends solely on ECS, we express TCR as a function of ECS by employing an equation from the literature that defines the relationship between TCR and ECS. Nijsse et al. (2020) demonstrated an approximate relationship between TCR and ECS based on CMIP6 and CMIP5 models given as ECS = TCR/(1 \u2013 e' \u00b7 TCR), with e' = 0.24 Nijsse et al. (2020).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "FaIR",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "temperature increase",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "where Sj(t) are the temperature compartments, whose sum gives a total temperature increase T(t), providing a mode representation of the energy balance model matrix (see Leach et al. for details).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GTAP-INT 2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USED_IN_EXPERIMENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "RCP6.0",
            "target_entity_type": "Experiment",
            "paragraph": "Unlike previous CGE-derived damage functions, which estimate damages at a specific year under varying temperature levels, we derive the damage function (black solid line) based on globally aggregated GDP losses simulated by the model for each year under the RCP6.0 pathway. This pathway was selected as it represents a mid-range emission-intensive scenario within our set.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
